WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:04.769
In this video, we'll look at how to set up your firewall and

2
00:00:04.769 --> 00:00:08.710
configure port forwarding to ensure that OSC works as expected.

3
00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:13.449
To start, let's look into our Windows Defender firewall settings.

4
00:00:13.449 --> 00:00:15.869
You will be able to find it in settings,

5
00:00:15.869 --> 00:00:20.109
and I'm also going to try to do it on my computer as well.

6
00:00:20.109 --> 00:00:22.489
First, I will go to settings,

7
00:00:22.489 --> 00:00:26.829
and it's already looking different because I'm now at Windows 11.

8
00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:31.850
Let me try to go to Windows Firewall.

9
00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:37.649
Windows Defender Firewall is the one we want to look into,

10
00:00:37.649 --> 00:00:39.509
so now let's go to here,

11
00:00:39.509 --> 00:00:41.310
and let's check two places.

12
00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:44.270
First, let's go to advanced settings here,

13
00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:51.130
and the place we want to see is the inbound and outbound rules.

14
00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:53.609
So for the inbound rules,

15
00:00:53.609 --> 00:00:56.929
I'm not going to specify any application,

16
00:00:56.929 --> 00:01:00.210
I'm just going to create a new one,

17
00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:05.430
new rule here, and I want to specify a port.

18
00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:09.230
I want to make sure the port is not blocked on this computer.

19
00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:12.560
So let's say I want to use,

20
00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:16.799
and another reminder is that OSC usually

21
00:01:16.799 --> 00:01:20.280
is going through UDP because it's a real-time protocol.

22
00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.439
So usually we will specify UDP,

23
00:01:22.439 --> 00:01:26.959
but there's no harm to also add one more rule for TCP.

24
00:01:26.959 --> 00:01:29.480
The port we're going to use today,

25
00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:32.760
and we're going to say it's just for example,

26
00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:35.560
I'm going to say it's 22222,

27
00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:39.849
the same as on the screenshot on the slide,

28
00:01:39.849 --> 00:01:47.310
and we want to use allow the connection.

29
00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:52.989
Next, and we will apply it to a domain and private and public.

30
00:01:52.989 --> 00:01:55.430
Next, and we'll give it a name,

31
00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:59.409
but because I already have this inbound and outbound rule set,

32
00:01:59.409 --> 00:02:01.890
I'm not going to finish the final part,

33
00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:07.730
but you can just name it something like 22222 for OSC,

34
00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:14.240
inbound, something like that.

35
00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.199
You can hit Finish to add the rule,

36
00:02:17.199 --> 00:02:20.409
and same thing goes to album rules.

37
00:02:20.409 --> 00:02:22.250
You can create a new rule,

38
00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:27.650
specify if you just want it to be open on a specific program,

39
00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:29.289
we can do it like this,

40
00:02:29.289 --> 00:02:31.289
and then we will specify the program,

41
00:02:31.289 --> 00:02:32.669
but in our use case,

42
00:02:32.669 --> 00:02:35.169
I'm going to specify the port.

43
00:02:35.169 --> 00:02:38.129
I want it for UDP,

44
00:02:38.129 --> 00:02:41.090
and I'll do it again for 22222,

45
00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:46.560
because I'm going to send message out from 22222 as well.

46
00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:50.520
Next, and I'll do allow the connection.

47
00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:54.810
Next, allow it on all three domains,

48
00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:58.689
and finally, I'll give it a name and finish it.

49
00:02:58.729 --> 00:03:02.449
Noting that if you're in a traditional local area network setup,

50
00:03:02.449 --> 00:03:06.129
you likely won't actually need to open the ports that you're using,

51
00:03:06.129 --> 00:03:08.009
but almost certainly you will if you're

52
00:03:08.009 --> 00:03:11.729
going across subnets or over the Internet.

53
00:03:11.729 --> 00:03:14.930
Now we make sure that this computer is not

54
00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:19.389
blocking the connection at our port 22222.

55
00:03:19.389 --> 00:03:23.569
Now we want to make sure when a message comes into the network,

56
00:03:23.569 --> 00:03:27.250
that message coming from 22222 is being forwarded

57
00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:31.000
to this computer that we just configured.

58
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:33.840
Let's go to our default gateway.

59
00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.560
The way to find our default gateway will be,

60
00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:39.080
let's press the Windows button,

61
00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:43.000
and let's type CMD for command prompt.

62
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:46.759
Now we open this command prompt,

63
00:03:46.759 --> 00:03:50.719
let's type in ipconfig.

64
00:03:50.719 --> 00:03:54.129
Now we have all these IPs here,

65
00:03:54.129 --> 00:03:57.849
and the one we want to look for is the default gateway.

66
00:03:57.969 --> 00:04:01.969
For me, it will be 192.168.1.1.

67
00:04:01.969 --> 00:04:05.169
Also a reminder that if you're looking for your local IP,

68
00:04:05.169 --> 00:04:07.490
it will be the IPv4 address here.

69
00:04:07.490 --> 00:04:11.569
For me, 192.168.1.245.

70
00:04:11.569 --> 00:04:12.810
So if you want to do

71
00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:17.970
a LAN communication with other device under the same network,

72
00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:22.769
this is the IP that represents my current computer.

73
00:04:22.769 --> 00:04:28.639
So I'm going to use my default gateway IP,

74
00:04:28.639 --> 00:04:31.399
and we will move on to the next part,

75
00:04:31.399 --> 00:04:35.560
to go to our router and set the port forwarding rules.

76
00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:38.329
Now let's move on to create

77
00:04:38.329 --> 00:04:42.529
a new port forwarding rule on the router.

78
00:04:42.529 --> 00:04:44.769
So this part is going to look very

79
00:04:44.769 --> 00:04:47.110
different from provider to provider,

80
00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:48.930
and for me, I'm using Optimum.

81
00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:51.569
So for Optimum users,

82
00:04:51.569 --> 00:04:53.610
this is the page you're going to see.

83
00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:56.329
First, we need to type in our Optimum ID and

84
00:04:56.329 --> 00:04:59.449
password to login and start creating new rules.

85
00:04:59.449 --> 00:05:05.410
For different users, you might open into a router setting page,

86
00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:08.209
you might need to put in passwords,

87
00:05:08.209 --> 00:05:09.889
it might be on your router.

88
00:05:09.889 --> 00:05:14.050
So please check your router and

89
00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:19.329
your Internet provider for how to access this setting page.

90
00:05:19.329 --> 00:05:23.009
So for all the Optimum users,

91
00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:26.769
let's look at my page for example.

92
00:05:26.769 --> 00:05:31.959
So here, I already logged into my Optimum account.

93
00:05:31.959 --> 00:05:34.040
I can see that I have

94
00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:37.800
one wired device and I have no Wi-Fi device,

95
00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:39.879
which is probably not true.

96
00:05:39.879 --> 00:05:42.600
So a reminder for Optimum users,

97
00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:44.959
you can always hit this refresh

98
00:05:44.959 --> 00:05:47.199
to get a new list of results here.

99
00:05:47.199 --> 00:05:49.199
A lot of time, it's not very sensitive.

100
00:05:49.199 --> 00:05:53.439
We have to refresh multiple times to have the correct result.

101
00:05:53.439 --> 00:05:56.879
But I'm only going to deal with the wired one here,

102
00:05:56.879 --> 00:05:59.779
so I'm going to leave it as is.

103
00:05:59.779 --> 00:06:03.639
We will scroll down to the port forwarding part,

104
00:06:03.639 --> 00:06:07.529
and I will hit Manage.

105
00:06:07.529 --> 00:06:11.389
So here, we can see that I already have several rules here,

106
00:06:11.389 --> 00:06:14.730
and including the rule 2222 here,

107
00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:21.850
I'm just going to delete it and I will do it again.

108
00:06:22.649 --> 00:06:25.730
So we deleted the port,

109
00:06:25.730 --> 00:06:27.689
and now we can use

110
00:06:27.689 --> 00:06:31.620
Add Port Forwarding Rule to create a new one.

111
00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:34.139
The process is very straightforward.

112
00:06:34.139 --> 00:06:36.300
Just select the device you want to use.

113
00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:40.379
Usually, it will show you the name of your computer.

114
00:06:40.379 --> 00:06:41.899
But if you don't see it,

115
00:06:41.899 --> 00:06:46.459
you can also come back to our command prompt and make sure

116
00:06:46.459 --> 00:06:49.980
the IPv4 address is matching the IPv4 address

117
00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:53.019
here to make sure they are the same device.

118
00:06:53.019 --> 00:06:56.000
I will collect my desktop,

119
00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:59.660
and the port I want to use is 2222.

120
00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:05.000
So now any messages coming from this port 2222 going

121
00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:10.720
to this Internet is going to be forwarded to my desktop here.

122
00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:13.480
The protocol is both TCP and UDP,

123
00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:18.079
but we can also specify it to be just TCP or just UDP.

124
00:07:18.079 --> 00:07:21.360
I'll leave it as is and save.

125
00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:24.680
Now, it is creating a new port forwarding rule.

126
00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:26.959
It might take a couple of seconds.

127
00:07:26.959 --> 00:07:29.959
Just let it run.

128
00:07:29.959 --> 00:07:33.379
After a couple of seconds,

129
00:07:33.379 --> 00:07:36.800
you should see a green thing shows

130
00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:41.600
that the port forwarding rule has successfully added.

131
00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:49.160
Now, you can see this new rule called 2222 for TCP and UDP.

132
00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:52.009
That's it.

133
00:07:52.009 --> 00:07:56.259
If you're not using your ISP as a router

134
00:07:56.259 --> 00:08:01.019
or otherwise don't have the ISP in control of your port forwarding,

135
00:08:01.019 --> 00:08:03.259
you might want to do it through an individual router.

136
00:08:03.259 --> 00:08:07.100
So I'll show you briefly here an example with a Netgear router.

137
00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:09.980
So in this case, I go to router login.net,

138
00:08:09.980 --> 00:08:11.360
which essentially takes me to

139
00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:14.500
the gateway that June already specified before.

140
00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:18.699
I can go over to advanced here and advanced setup.

141
00:08:18.699 --> 00:08:19.980
Again, like June mentioned,

142
00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:22.459
this will be different from router to router.

143
00:08:22.459 --> 00:08:24.779
We're just trying to show a couple of examples here.

144
00:08:24.779 --> 00:08:26.939
But if I go down to port forwarding,

145
00:08:26.939 --> 00:08:29.420
port triggering in this particular example,

146
00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:31.259
very similar to what we just saw,

147
00:08:31.259 --> 00:08:34.019
I can go ahead and add a new service.

148
00:08:34.019 --> 00:08:37.940
I've got to drop down here to specify what exactly it's for,

149
00:08:37.940 --> 00:08:41.940
or I can just type it in here as add custom service.

150
00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:45.850
So I can say that I want to open up port 2222,

151
00:08:46.009 --> 00:08:48.850
and I'll do the same thing for both internal and external,

152
00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:50.450
or if I hit that checkbox,

153
00:08:50.450 --> 00:08:56.690
it happens automatically, and I'll just call this OSC testing.

154
00:08:56.690 --> 00:08:58.850
Then I just need to make sure that I am

155
00:08:58.850 --> 00:09:02.169
specifying the correct internal IP address.

156
00:09:02.169 --> 00:09:04.419
Now, it's not clear from here

157
00:09:04.419 --> 00:09:06.899
which of these IP addresses is my device.

158
00:09:06.899 --> 00:09:09.539
I happen to know that it's 0.3

159
00:09:09.539 --> 00:09:13.480
because of using IP config and looking at the IPv4 address.

160
00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:15.700
So I would then just click apply,

161
00:09:16.139 --> 00:09:19.799
and now we have this new port forwarding rule

162
00:09:19.799 --> 00:09:22.460
ready to go through my separate router.

163
00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:24.940
Do note that sometimes you have to tell your ISP

164
00:09:24.940 --> 00:09:28.779
to specifically disable the router configuration on it.

165
00:09:28.779 --> 00:09:31.259
I actually was not able to use this port forwarding

166
00:09:31.259 --> 00:09:34.740
correctly until my ISP specifically disabled

167
00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:39.700
the router that was built into my modem.

168
00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:40.539
In the next video,

169
00:09:40.539 --> 00:09:43.259
we'll begin exploring the Unreal Engine sample project

170
00:09:43.259 --> 00:09:45.340
that we've put together for this course.
